 , cite9†Dimitra Simeonidou L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Multi-Agentic AI for Fairness-Aware and Accelerated Multi-modal Large Model Inference in Real-world Mobile Edge Networks, by Haiyuan Li and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Generative AI (GenAI) has transformed applications in natural language processing and content creation, yet centralized inference remains hindered by high latency, limited customizability, and privacy concerns. Deploying large models (LMs) in mobile edge networks emerges as a promising solution.\nL17: However, it also poses new challenges, including heterogeneous multi-modal LMs with diverse resource demands and inference speeds, varied prompt/output modalities that complicate orchestration, and resource-limited infrastructure ill-suited for concurrent LM execution. In response, we propose a Multi-Agentic AI framework for latency- and fairness-aware multi-modal LM inference in mobile edge networks.\nL18: Our solution includes a long-term planning agent, a short-term prompt scheduling agent, and multiple on-node LM deployment agents, all powered by foundation language models. These agents cooperatively optimize prompt routing and LM deployment through natural language reasoning over runtime telemetry and historical experience.\nL19: To evaluate its performance, we further develop a city-wide testbed that supports network monitoring, containerized LM deployment, intra-server resource management, and inter-server communications. Experiments demonstrate that our solution reduces average latency by over 80% and improves fairness (Normalized Jain index) to 0.90 compared to other baselines. Moreover, our solution adapts quickly without fine-tuning, offering a generalizable solution for optimizing GenAI services in edge environments.\nL20: Subjects:  | Systems and Control (eess.SY); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2602.07215 [eess.SY]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2602.07215v1 [eess.SY] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07215†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Haiyuan Li [cite15†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Fri, 6 Feb 2026 21:52:49 UTC (5,720 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Multi-Agentic AI for Fairness-Aware and Accelerated Multi-modal Large Model Inference in Real-world Mobile Edge Networks, by Haiyuan Li and 4 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite10†View PDF L36:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite16†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite17†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: eess.SY\nL43: \nL44: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L45: \nL46: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2026-02 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite23†cs L51: cite24†cs.AI L52: cite25†cs.DC L53: cite26†cs.SY L54: cite27†eess L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite28†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\nL71: \nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Bibliographic Explorer (cite31†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Connected Papers (cite32†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL84: \nL85: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL86: \nL87: Litmaps (cite33†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL88: \nL89: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL90: \nL91: scite Sm